<BaseLayout title="Venkateshwar Jambula — AI systems, engineering and research">
<section class="home-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Venkateshwar Jambula · Bengaluru</p><h1>I build systems that have to work.</h1>
<p class="lede">I’ve spent 15 years making complicated systems behave: engine ECUs, industrial optimization, Bixby, enterprise GenAI, and most recently long-running agents.</p>
<p class="secondary">I’m a hands-on AI engineer and technical leader, and I spent the last founder chapter building products from zero. These days I’m going deeper on agent reliability, formal verification, and the boundary between impressive demos and systems people can actually depend on.</p>
<div class="text-links"><a href="/work">work</a><a href="/notes">notes</a><a href="https://github.com/Venkat-RJ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github ↗</a><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/venkateshwar-jambula/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">linkedin ↗</a><a href="mailto:venkateshwar.jambula@gmail.com">email</a></div></div>
<figure class="portrait"><img src="/headshot-src.jpg" width="220" height="256" alt="Venkateshwar Jambula"/><figcaption>VENKAT / BENGALURU</figcaption></figure></section>

<section class="home-section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">Current question</p><h2>Reliability after the demo.</h2></div><p>The question that now connects my old embedded-systems instincts with the agentic systems I’m studying and building.</p></div>
<div class="current-question"><p class="question">How do we make increasingly autonomous AI systems remain coherent, inspectable and safe when they run for a long time, use tools, and act in the real world?</p>
<p class="why">This feels less like a new problem than it first appears. Embedded software taught me to think in invariants, failure modes, protocols and verification. Industrial ML taught me that offline accuracy is not the same thing as production reliability. Agents are forcing those two worlds back together.</p>
<p class="current-link">Current rabbit hole: <a href="https://github.com/cajal-technologies/talos/pull/249" target="_blank" rel="noopener">contributing to Talos, a Wasm interpreter in Lean ↗</a></p></div></section>

<section class="home-section"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">Selected artifacts</p><h2>Things you can inspect.</h2></div><p>I prefer links, code and concrete systems over a wall of adjectives.</p></div>
